speechtext:
The next amendment is:
That paragraph (j) of clause 2 of Bill 56 be struck out and the following substituted therefor:-*
"(j) 'property' means buildings, machinery, fixtures or other structures erected or placed upon, in, over, under or affixed to land, and all movable corporeal property in Canada, but shall not include:
(i)	land, exclusive of any structures aforesaid ;
(ii)	growing crops, plants and trees;
(iii)	ships or vessels other than ships being built or equipped in Canada prior to their being completed, launched and/or equipped;
(iv)	machinery, tackle, fishing gear or furniture of ships or vessels;
(v)	property or goods laden or freighted in ships or vessels other than goods laden or freiglited in ships used solely in inland wale rs;
(vi)	money, negotiable instruments, securities for money, evidence of title to any property or right or of the discharge of any obligation, or any documents owned for the purpose of a business;
(vii)	such other property as may be excluded by the terms of any contract of insurance or by any regulation
and does not include use and occupancy, profits, earnings, consequential loss, accounts, debts or any incorporeal property."
The main change that is being made by this amendment I have already explained to the house several times; that is, all ships are now taken out of the bill, but personal property, that is the goods laden or freighted in ships on inland waters, is still left in the bill. I have explained the reason for coming to that decision.
Before this amendment, paragraph (vi) in the printed bill included the words "does not include the use and occupancy of buildings," and so on. We have cut out the words "of buildings," because that is not necessary. Then we have put in that it does not include "consequential loss."
